#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/id #uuid "bbb751a9-ef0d-58cf-b788-18594dd67337" :question/math-academy-id "e-5560" :question/problem "For the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6}\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $H$ and $V$ as $\\{\\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2\\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ respectively.\n\nNow, we have to compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and the vectors in each set.\n\n- For the set $H$:\n$u \\cdot h_{1}|= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-6) + 6 \\cdot 1 = 0, ✓; u \\cdot h_{2}|= 3 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot (-3) + 6 \\cdot (-1) =-6 \\ne 0\\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ but $\\mathbf u \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_2$. Because $\\mathbf u$ is not orthogonal to every vector in $H$, we have $\\mathbf{u}\\not \\perp H$.\n\n- For the set $V$:\n$u \\cdot v_{1}|= 3 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 \\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}) = 0✓; u \\cdot v_{2}|= 3 \\cdot (-2) + 2 \\cdot 3 + 6 \\cdot 0 = 0✓$\nSo $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf u \\perp \\mathbf v_2$. Because $\\mathbf u$ is orthogonal to every vector in $V$, we have $\\mathbf{u}\\perp V$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "bbed6e9e-f63e-599f-ab8e-71cff46cfcfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5561" :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a+c}{b}$." :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_1=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}_2=0\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{u \\cdot w_{1} = 0; u \\cdot w_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=-3x_3$. So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3x_{3} \\\\ -x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_3=1$, we get the vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=-3$, $b=-1$, and $c=1$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a+c}{b}=\\dfrac{-3+1}{-1}=2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/id #uuid "ef531549-f5be-5002-b94f-1c7ee8682784" :question/math-academy-id "e-5562" :question/problem "Find $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2$.\n\nA vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_1=0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}\\cdot \\mathbf{h}_2=0\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{x \\cdot h_{1} = 0; x \\cdot h_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nWe write the corresponding augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} - \\frac{1}{2}R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd ones), so $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation we get\n\n$$\nx_2=-\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1 = -\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/id #uuid "b7cb0b20-e2a6-56d7-b568-ae08c259e581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5913" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{\\perp}$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors that span the subspace $H$ as $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2$.\n\nNow, a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to the set of vectors that spans the subspace $H$. So, we have to find the products $\\mathbf{v}_i \\cdot \\mathbf{h}_j$ for $i=1,2,3$ and $j=1,2$.\n\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_1$, we have the following:\n$v_{1} \\cdot h_{1}|= 3 \\cdot (-2) + 5 \\cdot (-1) + 4 \\cdot 3 = 1 \\ne 0 \\mid \\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf v_1 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1$, which means $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\notin H^{\\perp}$. We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_1\\cdot \\mathbf h_2$.\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_2$, we have the following:\n$v_{2} \\cdot h_{1}|= 2 \\cdot (-2) + 4 \\cdot (-1) + 4 \\cdot 3 = 4 \\ne 0 \\mid \\cdot$\nSo $\\mathbf v_2 \\not\\perp \\mathbf h_1$, which means $\\mathbf{v}_2 \\notin H^{\\perp}$. We do not need to test $\\mathbf v_2\\cdot \\mathbf h_2$.\n- For $\\mathbf{v}_3$, we have the following:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}v_{3} \\cdot h_{1}|= 5 \\cdot (-2) + 8 \\cdot (-1) + 6 \\cdot 3 = 0 & ✓ \\\\ v_{3} \\cdot h_{2}|= 5 \\cdot 2 + 8 \\cdot (-2) + 6 \\cdot 1 = 0 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_1$ and $\\mathbf v_3 \\perp \\mathbf h_2$, which means $\\mathbf{v}_3 \\in H^{\\perp}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{v}_3$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba6ce8b5-dfa2-5c1a-a946-4348e5f52ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131355} {:answer/id #uuid "d67bfc05-bfc7-56b7-a306-73a871b52bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131356} {:answer/id #uuid "38139241-031c-58ab-9e3c-457550a888fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131357} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3c986e-514b-55e2-8fd0-b3c026b9d0bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131358} {:answer/id #uuid "657357b8-88e7-5905-8483-5d07c8174ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d67bfc05-bfc7-56b7-a306-73a871b52bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131356} :answer-field/id #uuid "3997627d-56a3-5a4b-b56c-8e2492fb0655" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131354}] :question/id #uuid "7c796dfc-19c9-5431-bc95-d9fa1ccc2efa" :question/math-academy-id "q-39623" :question/problem "For the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $H = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n2. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $V = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d40cdb-97d6-52bf-a447-af5b54a22525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131367} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f45eea-b3b4-568b-8d08-3ebf845c33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131368} {:answer/id #uuid "c7981d7f-c4c4-5179-9dbb-4457ff8cb3b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131369} {:answer/id #uuid "e5842910-3cbd-5879-a73d-015215d3f586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131370} {:answer/id #uuid "23a0a656-e613-55d7-a396-26af793292d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f45eea-b3b4-568b-8d08-3ebf845c33cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131368} :answer-field/id #uuid "0842f212-ed40-522a-9d77-4d2d5c10f9ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131366}] :question/id #uuid "2175cca2-c03f-5359-9970-f5c376ea77b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39630" :question/problem "For the vector $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $u⊥H$, where $H = {\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$\n2. $u⊥V$, where $V = {\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. $u⊥W$, where $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6b3091c-1462-5a08-88f4-c47bf70f85e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131373} {:answer/id #uuid "e7cc2b80-2702-5c1a-bb82-01c19fc791dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131374} {:answer/id #uuid "f3cd9495-30b8-5068-a336-709d11e5471d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131375} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea5d17f-928a-5738-8f75-44a0a51f0662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131376} {:answer/id #uuid "78b8322b-069d-53e8-b8b4-943f90172823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6b3091c-1462-5a08-88f4-c47bf70f85e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131373} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e925a7b-21c1-508c-a6b0-151470cf3901" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131372}] :question/id #uuid "d9b7b645-faaf-50a2-b81a-1590fa3480cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39631" :question/problem "Consider the vector subspace $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}$. Which of the following vectors belong to $H^{⊥}$?\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "982be151-07af-5721-9b0b-145e3f9a5182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H^{⊥} = {0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131475} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef26135-2544-5c44-b46a-19a0cdb4cf64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H^{⊥} = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{2} ∈ R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131476} {:answer/id #uuid "bd383cc1-2365-5056-94a4-2c6d7264c473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H^{⊥} = {\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{1} ∈ R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131477} {:answer/id #uuid "f51d3bec-f11a-56be-b092-6daf15645b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H^{⊥} = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{2} ∈ R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131478} {:answer/id #uuid "2be02389-22d9-5eb9-9232-9f6875bb0697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H^{⊥} = {\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 3x_{1}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{1} ∈ R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f51d3bec-f11a-56be-b092-6daf15645b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H^{⊥} = {\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}: x_{2} ∈ R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131478} :answer-field/id #uuid "e323ffd5-1c90-594e-a314-9458280b0a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131474}] :question/id #uuid "17bd69d7-65e4-535e-bfc3-813ca9368a10" :question/math-academy-id "q-39915" :question/problem "Find $H^{⊥}$ if $H = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "842b5c5c-cc5c-50d2-b823-6def6fca04e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132957} {:answer/id #uuid "da8821b3-5d06-5e21-80f5-a553896d64c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132958} {:answer/id #uuid "15e8b357-5098-57b9-8c74-02a2282b9714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132959} {:answer/id #uuid "38f6361e-a902-560f-9217-4c195d638701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132960} {:answer/id #uuid "660803b9-f7f4-568d-8a7c-5d45e4320d32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15e8b357-5098-57b9-8c74-02a2282b9714" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132959} :answer-field/id #uuid "142bdc42-c623-5b18-9590-d0d791655eb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132956}] :question/id #uuid "e055065d-3cdf-5f06-8b7f-a22a1c681c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-71102" :question/problem "Given that $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$ is a non-zero vector that is orthogonal to the set $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$, find the value of $\\frac{c}{a + b}$."}]}
